  "response": "When a hospitalized patient presents with acute liver injury and both a medication and an intrinsic disease could explain the laboratory picture, the clinician's task is less to prove one cause than to dismantle the others. Drug-induced liver injury is a diagnosis of exclusion, and because of this, the Roussel Uclaf Causality Assessment Method (RUCAM) is a scoring tool used to assess causality in suspected drug-induced liver injury. It converts the work of exclusion into a structured inventory, assigning points for the timing of exposure, the course after withdrawal, risk factors, concomitant drugs, the search for alternative causes, prior reports of hepatotoxicity, and response to rechallenge. Whereas the instrument now sits at the center of routine hepatology practice, RUCAM was first published in 2010.\n\nBefore any score can be calculated, the injury must be classified by pattern, because the competing diagnoses and the RUCAM criteria differ by category. The classification rests on the R ratio, defined as serum ALT divided by its upper limit of normal, over alkaline phosphatase divided by its upper limit of normal. An R ratio of 5 or greater indicates a hepatocellular pattern of liver injury. By contrast, an R ratio below 2 indicates a hepatocellular pattern of liver injury. Values falling between these thresholds define a mixed pattern, and the assigned category determines which time windows for enzyme improvement are applied later in the assessment.\n\nThe exclusion phase then turns to viral, autoimmune, vascular, and metabolic disease, and acute hepatitis E deserves particular attention because it is frequently overlooked in older inpatients. In the case under discussion, the serology admits no ambiguity of structure: either the patient's hepatitis E IgM test result was positive, or the patient's hepatitis E IgM test result was negative. The two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold, so the result either keeps a viral explanation open or closes it. Serologic panels for hepatitis A, B, and C, together with Doppler imaging of the hepatic vasculature and a review of alcohol intake, complete this stage of the workup and narrow the field of credible alternatives.\n\nThe medication history supplies the temporal half of the argument. The patient took amoxicillin-clavulanate for pneumonia in the weeks before hospital admission, and this exposure preceded the subsequent course in which the patient's ALT fell by more than half within 30 days after amoxicillin-clavulanate was stopped. In other words, within 30 days of discontinuing amoxicillin-clavulanate, the patient's ALT dropped below 50% of its peak value. This dechallenge response is weighted heavily in RUCAM scoring for hepatocellular injury. Improvement of liver enzymes after stopping a suspected drug supports a diagnosis of drug-induced liver injury, as is confirmed by this patient's outcome, for the ALT decline indicates that the patient had a drug-induced liver injury. Yet the presence of one mechanism does not preclude another: at least one of these held, perhaps both, namely the drug-induced injury, or the patient had underlying autoimmune hepatitis. Autoantibody titers and elevated immunoglobulin G kept the second process firmly in view.\n\nDisagreement among specialists is common at precisely this juncture, since autoimmune hepatitis can be unmasked or mimicked by a drug. Although the consulting rheumatologist argued that the patient's liver injury reflected autoimmune hepatitis, the attribution to amoxicillin-clavulanate held, because the independent expert adjudication panel determined that autoimmune hepatitis was not the cause of the patient's liver injury. That determination settled the competing interpretations: the autoimmune serology described a background condition, while the injury itself followed the drug. Expert adjudication of this kind, which integrates histology, the trajectory after withdrawal, and the full exclusion record, remains the reference standard against which scoring instruments are judged, and it is the step clinicians rely on when structured scores and specialist opinion fail to converge.",
